## Failure map

| Failure mode | [E#]/[D#] | Who suffers | Matters here? |
|---|---|---|---|
| No dataset is supplied, so there is no data-derived failure. Evaluation is the attack suite. | [P15] [A4] | Any approach that claims a "score" without a defined attack suite | Yes. The X1 attack-suite pass rate must be defined up front |
| Off-UserOp spend path. If the account's ERC-1271 accepts the agent key, EIP-3009 `transferWithAuthorization` (x402 "exact") and Permit2 pulls move funds without any UserOp, so a policy that only lives in validateUserOp/execute never sees them | [E20] [E21] [E22] [E26] [E7] | Every 4337 session-key design that guards only UserOps; x402 payers using smart accounts | Yes, top. x402 API credits are a named use case (R13), and permit-style signatures were 38% of large 2025 phishing losses [E26] |
| Agent-side and model-level defenses fail. Adaptive attacks bypass 12 defenses at >90% ASR for most of them; memory injection beats prompt-injection detectors on Web3 agents; web agents are partially hijacked in up to 86% of cases | [E39] [E23] [E28] [E29] [E30] [E24] | Agent-harness guards (CaMeL, detectors), client-side policy middleware [E38], prompt-level budgets | Yes. The wallet must hold with a fully hijacked agent (R3, R15) |
| Off-chain policy engines trust a provider, TEE or app server, so a leaked key or compromised host bypasses them | [E18] [E19] [E38] [E35] | Coinbase CDP policies, Privy, presidio-hardened-x402, the user's own sats_router/trace-api caps | Yes (R3, R14). This is the gap on-chain enforcement closes |
| Standards specify the request/delegation surface but no enforcement semantics. ERC-7715 defines only an expiry rule; ERC-7710 defines no caveats; combining caveats is left to the integrator, so approve-laundering (T3) and split-across-delegations (T6) are uncovered by default | [E11] [E12] [E13] [E15] | Dapps and agents relying on 7715/7710 alone | Yes. The value we add is a composed, tested enforcement module behind a 7715-shaped request |
| Periodic-bucket resets instead of a rolling window allow 2x spend at the boundary (T7); some caps are lifetime, not daily | [E14] [E17] [E6] [E8] | Coinbase Spend Permissions, Safe Allowance Module, Kernel (no rolling value cap), Alchemy MAv2 (lifetime limits) | Yes (R6) |
| Recipient hidden in calldata (T4). Only Zodiac Roles checks per-parameter conditions, and it is a Safe module rather than 4337-native; target-only allowlists miss the ERC-20 `to` | [E16] [E40] [E18] | Target/contract allowlists (allowance.eth, CDP evmAddress) | Yes (R7) |
| ERC-7562 validation rules ban TIMESTAMP/NUMBER and limit storage, so expiry must go through validUntil and the daily window must be enforced in execution | [E1] [E2] | Naive validators that read block.timestamp or external state during validation (bundlers reject them) | Yes. This is a design constraint (T1 confirmed) |
| Replay and binding: the userOpHash differs between EntryPoint v0.7 and v0.8; EIP-7702 chainId-0 authorizations replay across chains, and the EntryPoint can re-activate delegated code without the victim; the Smart Sessions enable-in-first-UserOp flow is a risk point | [E5] [E4] [E41] [E7] | Wallets that mix EntryPoint versions or use full 7702 delegation for agents | Yes (R11, T8). Pin one EntryPoint version; use a scoped session key, not 7702 full delegation |
| x402 protocol-level flaws: duplicate-settlement races, allowance overdraft, cross-resource substitution, binding/replay weaknesses | [E36] [E37] | Mostly merchants and facilitators; payers through overdraft and paid-but-denied outcomes | Partly. The wallet bounds payer loss, but it does not fix facilitators |
| Injection benchmarks mock their finance tools, with no on-chain settlement, so ASR is not the same as funds lost | [E27] [E29] [E23] [E28] | Every benchmark-only security claim | Yes (R16). Map each attack to a tx outcome (reverted / bounded loss) |
| Trust propagation amplifies Sybil attacks (EigenTrust routes 81.7% of jobs to malicious agents vs 29.0% for reputation-only). Prior trace-api code deny-lists only HOLD/INVESTIGATE and fails open on a missing score (research_parts/prior_work.md, extension.py:105-107) | [E34] | Any trust-score-gated recipient policy | Only if TRACE is used: allow-list ROUTE* only, and the score may escalate to owner approval but never loosens on-chain caps |
| In-policy overpay (T10) and approval fatigue/misleading requests (T11) | no source found | Any capped design | Yes. Claim bounded loss, not zero loss; tag as an assumption in phase C |

